 Finding _facts_ is your job, never the user's. When a frontier question needs a fact from the environment (filesystem, tools, etc.), dispatch a sub-agent to find it; don't ask the user for anything you could look up yourself. Don't block on it: a running exploration is an unsettled prerequisite, so only the questions downstream of it wait for the sub-agent to report; ask the rest of the frontier now. The _decisions_ are the user's: put each to them and wait.
 
+
+## Working From a Brainstorming Handoff
+
+When you start from a requirements spec produced by the brainstorming skill (`docs/specs/*-requirements.md`):
+
+- The spec's requirements, constraints, and success criteria are settled product decisions. Treat them as root constraints of the design tree — do not re-interrogate them. If a requirement makes the design tree infeasible, say so explicitly instead of silently re-litigating it.
+- The spec's final **Open technical decisions** section seeds the first frontier: each listed decision becomes a top-level node; derive its dependent decisions from it.
+- When the frontier empties, the settled tree is the technical solution record. Summarize it alongside the spec so the pair (requirements + settled decisions) is the complete design.
+
+
 The session is done when the frontier is empty: every branch of the design tree visited, nothing left silently assumed. Do not act on it until the user confirms you have reached a shared understanding.
